\title{The JVM launcher line, and no AOT cache}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/J1.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. The line is generated by \src{scripts/build.sh} since \commit{ca8e886}.}

\p{\strong{Context.} When warm wall clock became the metric, the JVM launcher became [the platform of the metric](dec-WX9Z). Its flags dated from the time when instructions were the target: two compiler threads, because the arena bills compiler threads, and a fixed heap of 13 GB sized for the reference engine. A profile of the JVM platform (report J1) asked whether the steady state is as good as HotSpot can do, how fast the VM gets there and whether it can start warm, how much memory the machine needs, and what the launcher should ship. Conditions of every number below: HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 on the aarch64 development host, machine engine with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, 8 workers unless said otherwise, load pipelined, every pass a complete run, every timing inside a quiet-core window. Two identical 8-worker rows differ by up to 5 \% on that shared host; a difference below that is not a finding.}

\p{\strong{Decision.} The launcher passes the following flags, and a heap chosen by the engine of the run. There is no AOT cache and no class-data archive; the JIT is C2, not Graal; the tiers stay on.}

\pre{\verb~|
-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=8 -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages
-Xlog:disable -Xlog:all=warning:stderr -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow
heap, machine:           -Xmx7g -Xmn1g           (no -Xms)
heap, any other engine:  -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g
~}

\p{\strong{Evidence.}}

\table{
\tr{\th{Change}\th{Measured}}
\tr{\td{compiler threads, 2 to 8}\td{With 2, a tiered VM has one C2 thread beside eight workers and needs four to six passes. With 8 the cold wall clock falls from 4.1-5.3 to 2.8-3.6 s on Init, 5.5 to 3.1 s on Std, 8.5-9.9 to 5.2-5.8 s on cedar and 4.0 to 2.5 s on reachability, and the third pass is steady. Nothing is lost once warm: warm wall of the new line over the old is 0.94 (Init), 1.03 (Std), 0.88 (cedar), 1.00 (reachability). The threads are created on demand, so 8 is a cap.}}
\tr{\td{transparent huge pages}\td{The only flag with a consistent steady-state gain. Mathlib in an A B A B (2 passes): second pass 22.56 and 21.87 s with, 24.01 and 23.10 s without, that is -6 \% warm wall and -9 \% on the first pass. Reachability -2 to -4 \%, cedar 0 to -4 \%.}}
\tr{\td{VM messages to standard error}\td{The VM prints its own warnings on standard output by default.}}
\tr{\td{machine heap 7 GB, no minimum}\td{The warm pass over Mathlib takes 23.94 / 24.07 / 24.21 / 23.99 s under 13 / 8 / 7 / 6 GB; the peak resident set is 8.0-8.7 GB under 13 GB and 5.7-6.7 GB under 7 GB. 7 GB is the smallest cap at which the machine decides every unit it decides at 13 GB. Under 6 GB its own heap rule refuses 2 to 6 session growths per pass (finding J1-F1: it assumes a young reserve of a third of the heap whatever the nursery flag says); the driver's cold re-check then decides those units and the verdict is unchanged. Under 5 GB with the same nursery the run ends with an out-of-memory error, status 3.}}
\tr{\td{every other engine keeps 13 GB}\td{The reference engine has 13.4 GB resident on Mathlib; it was not measured with another heap here.}}
}

\p{\strong{What does not move the warm pass.} C2 compiles all of the machine; nothing bails out and nothing hot is left in a lower tier. It runs into its inlining budget in five to twelve of about 940 compile units, and neither lifting nor tightening the limits that are flags changes the warm pass beyond the noise. Nor do Graal as the top tier (1.98 s against 1.99-2.10 s on cedar, with 400 MB more memory), compact object headers or lower tier thresholds. C2 without the C1 tiers gains 0 to 7 \% with one worker and nothing with 8. The hottest frames are probes into session tables: the time is in memory, not in code shape.}

\p{\strong{Why no AOT cache.} The AOT cache of JDK 25 works on GraalVM CE 25 only with JVMCI switched off. Trained on Std with four passes it is 18.7 MB and needs one training run at build time. With 8 compiler threads it takes another 0.5 s off the cold wall on Init (2.33 against 2.87 s) and 1.1 s on cedar (4.69 against 5.75 s), and it makes every later pass slower: warm wall with the cache over without is 1.06 and 1.09 on Init, 1.07 on Std, the training corpus itself, and 1.05 on reachability with one worker; 11 of 11 comparisons point the same way. Warm wall clock is the metric, so half a second at the start does not buy a loss of 5 to 9 \% on every pass. A reading from one diagnostic pair, not a proof: the VM compiles at once from the mature profile and has no reason to compile again, and that code is larger (3.43 MB against 2.65 MB). A classic class-data archive, and the cache without its profiles, gain nothing: class loading is 0.1 to 0.2 s of a start. With the cache the VM also cannot run Graal, so [the Truffle tier](design-truffle-tier) would have no compiler.}

\p{\strong{Consequences.} The launcher reads the engine of the run from its arguments, see [the launchers](design-launchers). The machine's heap can go to 6 GB, 5.8 GB resident at the same wall clock, once its young reserve follows the real young generation; that change was proposed and not tested. The cache remains an option for a launcher that is known to run once. The collector was a separate question, see [dec-Z6DD](dec-Z6DD).}

\p{\strong{Not established.} Effects below about 5 \%; x86-64, the architecture of the arena runner; a host whose transparent huge pages are set to \code{never}; one worker on Mathlib.}

\p{Documents: \src{docs/research/profiles/J1.md}, \src{docs/PLATFORM.md}. Source: [the session of the profiles and prototypes](ses-JRSD).}
